{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a thought-provoking film that delves into the complexities of friendship, ambition, and the consequences of success. directed by david finch erdman, the movie tells the story of mark zuckerberg, the co-founder and former ceo of facebook, and his journey to building one of the world's most influential social media platforms.\n\nthe film begins with mark's (jesse eisenberg) college days at harvard, where he meets his future business partner and best friend, eduardo saverin (andrew garfield). their initial intention is to create a website that allows harvard students to connect with each other, but soon their vision expands to create something much bigger.\n\nthe film explores the intricate dynamics of mark and eduardo's relationship, showcasing the ups and downs of their friendship as they navigate the challenges of entrepreneurship. as the story progresses, mark's character evolves, and we see him become increasingly consumed by his own ambition, often at the expense of those around him.\n\nthe supporting cast, including duncan harris (robin arounslow), a fellow harvard student who becomes involved in the facebook project, adds depth and nuance to the narrative. the film also shines a light on the role of sebastian thrun (bassem tarabouche), a harvard professor who helps mark and eduardo develop their idea.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its attention to detail. the movie is meticulously researched, with a keen eye for accuracy in its depiction of the early days of facebook. the cinematography is also noteworthy, capturing the essence of the harvard campus and the rapidly changing world of social media.\n\nthe performances in the film are superb, with jesse eisenberg delivering a critically acclaimed portrayal of mark zuckerberg. andrew garfield and armie hamilton also shine as eduardo and duncan, respectively. the 